---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import SeriesNav from '../../components/SeriesNav.astro';

export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'Attention without a KV cache',
  pubDate: '2026-08-16',
  description: 'What the KV cache actually costs, why the softmax is the thing that forbids a fixed-size memory, and how to build one instead.',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <SeriesNav current="linear-attention" />

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    To generate one more token, attention re-reads every key and value it has stored for every token
    that came before it. That is why it recalls things precisely — nothing was ever thrown away — and
    it is also why a long context costs gigabytes of GPU memory that have to be dragged across the
    memory bus once per token generated.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    This part works out what that actually costs, then builds the alternative: one fixed-size matrix,
    updated as you read, instead of a transcript that grows forever. By the end you will have a working
    memory that is the same size at token 10 as at token 10 million — and a clear view of the three
    specific ways it breaks, which are the subject of the next three parts.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    You need to know what attention does. Everything else gets built here. The figures are live, so
    drag them, and the dotted terms open short asides you can skip.
  </p>

  <section id="bill" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">1. The bill comes due</h2>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      Dotted terms open a short aside. Everything in them is detail you can skip — the main line reads straight through.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Softmax attention has a property that is easy to admire and expensive to keep: <b>it never
      compresses anything</b>. To produce token <span class="font-mono text-sm">t+1</span> it needs the
      key and value vectors of every earlier token, in full, unmodified. That is exactly why it is so
      good at precise recall — nothing was thrown away — and it is also the entire problem, because
      those vectors have to be
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">read</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Read from where, exactly?</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The cached keys and values sit in the GPU's <b>HBM</b> — the multi-gigabyte
        pool <span class="m">nvidia-smi</span> reports. The arithmetic happens somewhere far smaller and
        faster: the on-chip SRAM and registers of each streaming multiprocessor, tens of megabytes at
        most.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">So "read" means <b>copied out of HBM, across the memory bus, into on-chip
        memory</b> — once per layer, and again for every token you generate, because there is nowhere
        on-chip to keep a multi-gigabyte cache between steps.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">Why it matters: on an H100 the arithmetic units can perform roughly <b>500
        floating-point operations in the time it takes to fetch one number from HBM</b>. Whichever of the
        two you need more of sets your speed.</span
      ></span></span>
      back for every single token generated.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Attention is charged for twice, in two different currencies. Digesting the prompt costs
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">O(T²) of arithmetic, once</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Prefill.</b> You have <span class="m">T</span> queries, and each attends to
        up to <span class="m">T</span> keys, each comparison a <span class="m">d</span>-dimensional dot
        product. That is <span class="m">T × T × d</span> multiply-adds, so
        <span class="m">O(T²d)</span>.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">It is <b>quadratic, and it is compute</b>. Every query is independent, so it
        parallelises beautifully — you are simply buying a lot of arithmetic. Double the prompt,
        quadruple the work.</span
      ></span></span>;
      generating each new token costs
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">O(T) of memory traffic, every time</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Decoding.</b> Now there is exactly <em>one</em> query. It attends to
        <span class="m">T</span> keys, so the arithmetic is only <span class="m">O(Td)</span> —
        trivial.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">But to do it you must fetch all <span class="m">T</span> keys and all
        <span class="m">T</span> values out of HBM: <span class="m">2Td</span> numbers moved to perform
        about <span class="m">2Td</span> operations. <b>Roughly one operation per number fetched</b>,
        against hardware that wants five hundred. The units idle, waiting on memory.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">So decoding is <b>bandwidth-bound and linear in T per token</b> — and
        generating <span class="m">N</span> tokens drags <span class="m">O(N·T)</span> bytes across the
        bus.</span
      ></span></span>.
      Long-context generation is not slow because the maths is hard. It is slow because the same
      gigabytes get hauled through the same bus, over and over.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      And those gigabytes are not a figure of speech. For a 7B-class model in fp16,
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">a 2048-token context costs exactly 1 GiB</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Count the numbers.</b> Two tensors — one key, one value — per token, per
        layer, per key/value head:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">KV bytes = 2 · L · H<sub>kv</sub> · d<sub>h</sub> · T · p</span
        ><span class="kn-legend"
          ><span class="kn-hd">symbol</span><span class="kn-hd">what it is</span><span class="kn-hd">here</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">2</span><span>one key <em>and</em> one value stored per token — the only constant in the formula</span><span class="kn-val">2</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">L</span><span>layers that keep a cache. In a plain transformer that is every block</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">H<sub>kv</sub></span><span>key/value heads per layer. Equals <span class="m">H</span> unless the model shares them</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">H</span><span>query heads per layer. Does <em>not</em> enter the cache size — only <span class="m">H<sub>kv</sub></span> does</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>h</sub></span><span>width of one head, so <span class="m">H · d<sub>h</sub></span> = the model width (4096 here)</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">T</span><span>tokens currently in the context — <b>the one that grows</b></span><span class="kn-val">2,048</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">p</span><span>bytes per stored number, i.e. the precision</span><span class="kn-val">2</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Those are not invented: for any model on the Hub they are
        <span class="m">num_hidden_layers</span>, <span class="m">num_key_value_heads</span>,
        <span class="m">num_attention_heads</span> and
        <span class="m">hidden_size / num_attention_heads</span> in its
        <span class="m">config.json</span>. Substituting the 7B shape above, in fp16, at
        <span class="m">T</span> = 2048:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">2 × 32 × 4096 × 2048 × 2<br/>= 1,073,741,824 B = <b>1.000 GiB</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Linear in <span class="m">T</span>, so 32K tokens costs 16 GiB and 128K costs
        64 GiB — for a model whose <em>weights</em> are 13.5 GiB.</span
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>p</b> is the one factor you can change without touching the architecture,
        which is why quantised caches are everywhere:</span
        ><span class="kn-tbl"
          ><span class="kn-hd">Precision</span><span class="kn-hd">p</span><span class="kn-hd">2048 tokens</span
          ><span>fp32</span><span>4</span><span>2.00 GiB</span
          ><span>bf16 / fp16</span><span>2</span><span>1.00 GiB</span
          ><span>fp8</span><span>1</span><span>0.50 GiB</span
          ><span>int4</span><span>0.5</span><span>0.25 GiB</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p" style="margin-top:8px">Note <span class="m">H<sub>kv</sub></span>, not
        <span class="m">H</span>: that is <b>grouped-query attention</b>. Let several query heads share
        one key/value head and the cache shrinks by exactly that ratio — Llama-3-70B uses 64 query heads
        against 8 KV heads, turning 5.00 GiB into 0.62 GiB.</span
      ></span></span>
      of cache. Quantisation and head-sharing attack the constants in that formula, and they help a lot
      in practice — but they leave the <span class="font-mono text-sm">T</span> exactly where it is. That
      is the factor linear attention goes after.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The rest of this post builds a mechanism whose memory is
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">a fixed-size matrix instead — 32 MiB whether the context is 2K or 2M</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p">The same count, for one <span class="m">d<sub>k</sub> × d<sub>v</sub></span>
        state per head per layer. Note what is <em>missing</em>:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq kn-eq-ok">state bytes = L · H · d<sub>k</sub> · d<sub>v</sub> · p&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>← no T</b><br/><br/>32 × 32 × 128 × 128 × 2<br/>= 33,554,432 B = <b>32 MiB</b></span
        ><span class="kn-legend"
          ><span class="kn-sym">L, H, p</span><span>as above — layers, heads per layer, bytes per number</span><span class="kn-val">32, 32, 2</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>k</sub></span><span>key width: the <em>rows</em> of the state matrix, one per key channel</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>v</sub></span><span>value width: the <em>columns</em>. So each head holds one <span class="m">d<sub>k</sub> × d<sub>v</sub></span> matrix</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Setting the two formulas equal gives the crossover: below about <b>64
        tokens</b> the fixed state is the <em>bigger</em> object. Above it, the KV cache runs away and
        never comes back.</span
      ></span></span>.
      The figure below is just those two formulas with a slider on them.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="cache-growth"></div>
  </section>


  <section id="parentheses" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">2. Why the softmax has to go</h2>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      We want a fixed-size summary instead of a growing cache. Here is what stands in the way. Attention
      for one query, in full:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      O = softmax( Q Kᵀ / √d ) V
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Three matrices. And matrix multiplication is <em>associative</em> — normally you may bracket a
      product any way you like and get the same answer. If we could compute
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">Kᵀ V</span> first, we would never build the
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">T×T</span> object at all; we would build a
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> one that does not grow with the sequence. That
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> object is exactly the fixed-size summary we are after.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>The softmax is the one thing that forbids it.</b> It works
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">row by row across the whole T×T matrix</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Why that blocks the regrouping.</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Softmax turns a row of scores into weights that sum to 1 by dividing each by
        the total:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">softmax(s)<sub>j</sub> = e<sup>s<sub>j</sub></sup> / Σ<sub>i</sub> e<sup>s<sub>i</sub></sup></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Look at the denominator. To know <em>any single weight</em> you need the sum
        over the entire row — every score that query produced against every key. So the row has to exist
        before you can normalise it, and all <span class="m">T</span> rows make the
        <span class="m">T×T</span> matrix.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">That is what "non-linear" costs you here. A linear function
        <span class="m">f</span> would satisfy <span class="m">f(QKᵀ)V = Q·f(KᵀV)</span>-style
        rearrangement; <span class="m">exp</span> and the division do not commute with the matrix
        product, so the brackets are frozen where they are.</span
      ></span></span>,
      so you cannot know a single attention weight without first computing that query's score against
      every key. The <span class="font-mono text-sm">T×T</span> matrix is not an implementation choice —
      the softmax <em>requires</em> it to exist. Which means the growing cache is not really attention's
      fault. It is the softmax's.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      So the move is to take it out. Not because it is bad at its job — it is very good at its job — but
      because it is the only thing between us and a memory that does not grow:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      O = (Q Kᵀ) V &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;⟶&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; O = Q (Kᵀ V)
      <span class="block mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600 font-sans">with nothing non-linear in between, the brackets are free to move</span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">But it was doing two jobs. Where did they go?</h3>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is the part I glossed over for far too long, and it matters, because deleting the softmax is
      not free — it was earning its place.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Job one: keeping the output bounded.</b> Softmax weights are non-negative and sum to exactly 1,
      so the output is a weighted <em>average</em> of value vectors — it can never be larger than the
      largest value you put in. Delete it and you have a bare sum that grows as you add terms. That job
      was <b>reassigned</b>, and
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">you can see exactly where in KDA</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Normalisation moved out of the attention and around it.</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The original linear attention (Katharopoulos et al., 2020) kept an explicit
        denominator — it divided by <span class="m">φ(q)ᵀ Σ φ(k)</span> to restore the average. Modern
        variants dropped that and use norms instead. In KDA, from <a href="/blog/delta-rule#overwriting" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">part 2</a> of the paper:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">q, k = <b>L2Norm</b>(Swish(ShortConv(W x)))<br/>o&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; = W<sub>o</sub>( gate ⊙ <b>RMSNorm</b>( attention output ) )</span
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>L2Norm</b> on <span class="m">q</span> and <span class="m">k</span> forces
        every score to be a cosine in <span class="m">[−1, 1]</span>, so no single comparison can be
        huge. <b>RMSNorm</b> on the way out rescales whatever the state produced. And the decay gate that
        arrives in <a href="/blog/gated-deltanet" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">part 3</a> stops the state itself from accumulating without limit.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">Three bounded things in place of one. The paper's Table 6 quietly admits this
        by noting it "omitted the normalization term" to keep the equations readable — the norms are
        there in the real layer.</span
      ></span></span>.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Job two: sharpening.</b> The <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp</span> in softmax is
      <em>exponential</em>: a score of 8 against a score of 6 does not get 8/6 of the weight, it gets
      about 7× the weight. That turns a soft weighted average into something close to a
      <b>hard lookup of the single best-matching key</b> — which is precisely what makes attention so
      good at fishing one exact fact out of a long context.
    </p>

    <div class="my-5 p-4 rounded border-l-4 border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 text-gray-800">
      <b>That job was not reassigned. It was given up.</b> Nothing in linear attention sharpens; every
      stored pair contributes in proportion to its raw key overlap, forever. This is not a detail — it is
      the root of the crowding in <a href="/blog/delta-rule#crowding" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">part 2</a>, the reason a fixed-size memory blurs facts together, and ultimately
      why Kimi Linear keeps one real attention layer in every four rather than going fully linear. When
      you read <a href="/blog/kda#results" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">part 4</a> and see full attention winning on precise recall, this paragraph is why.
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Now the algebra</h3>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      With the softmax gone, one query's output is just a weighted sum with unnormalised weights:
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">o<sub>t</sub> = Σ<sub>j≤t</sub> (q<sub>t</sub>·k<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>j</sub></span>.
      Three lines rearrange that into a state —
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">the sum moves inside the brackets</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><span class="m">q<sub>t</sub>·k<sub>j</sub></span> is a <em>scalar</em>, so it
        can be slid around freely:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">o<sub>t</sub> = Σ<sub>j</sub> (q<sub>t</sub>ᵀk<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>j</sub><br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= Σ<sub>j</sub> v<sub>j</sub> (k<sub>j</sub>ᵀq<sub>t</sub>)<br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= ( Σ<sub>j</sub> v<sub>j</sub>k<sub>j</sub>ᵀ ) q<sub>t</sub></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The last step is the whole trick: <span class="m">q<sub>t</sub></span> is the
        same in every term, so it factors out — and the sum over past tokens ends up
        <em>inside</em> the brackets, where it no longer involves the query at all.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">In attention you compare against every key and then combine. Here you combine
        all the keys and values first, once, and compare a single time at the end.</span
      ></span></span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> + k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; o<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t</sub>ᵀ q<sub>t</sub>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The causal mask did not disappear either — it <b>became "the running total so far"</b>. And the KV
      cache is gone: in its place is one matrix per head, the same size at token 10 as at token 10
      million. This is <b>linear attention</b> (Katharopoulos et al., 2020).
    </p>

    <div data-widget="associativity"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Note the crossover in that figure: below roughly <span class="font-mono text-sm">T ≈ 3d</span>,
      quadratic attention is the <em>cheaper</em> option. Linear attention is not a free lunch, it is a
      different asymptote — one you only start collecting on at long context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 3 -->

  <section id="memory" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">3. That state is a lookup table</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Before anything goes wrong, let's be concrete about what <span class="font-mono text-sm">S</span>
      <em>is</em>. Take a two-dimensional example and write two pairs into it:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      k₁ = [1, 0]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v₁ = [5, 0]<br/>
      k₂ = [0, 1]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v₂ = [0, 7]<br/><br/>
      S = k₁v₁ᵀ + k₂v₂ᵀ = [[5, 0],&nbsp;&nbsp;+&nbsp;&nbsp;[[0, 0],&nbsp;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&nbsp;[[5, 0],<br/>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 0]]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 7]]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 7]]
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Now read it back with <span class="font-mono text-sm">q = k₁</span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-green-200 bg-green-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      Sᵀq = [5, 0] = v₁ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="font-sans text-green-800">exactly what was stored</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      So <b>S is a lookup table built by addition</b>. Writing is "add an outer product"; reading is
      "multiply by a key". That is the whole mechanism, and it is the object every remaining idea in
      this post modifies.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      It worked perfectly there for one reason: <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₁</span> and
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₂</span> are perpendicular. Take that away and things break —
      in <em>two separate ways</em> that are worth keeping apart, because they have different fixes
      and only one of them is the delta rule's.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section id="three-problems" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">4. Three ways this breaks</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      That lookup table worked perfectly for one reason: <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₁</span> and
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₂</span> were perpendicular. Take that away and it breaks — in
      three separate ways that are worth keeping strictly apart, because they have three different
      fixes and only one of them is the famous one.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Problem</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">What goes wrong</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">What fixes it</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Overwriting</td><td class="pr-4">re-writing a key adds to it instead of replacing</td><td>the delta rule — completely (part 2)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Crowding</td><td class="pr-4">more keys than the state has perpendicular directions</td><td>nothing but a bigger state (part 2)</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-blue-50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Staleness</td><td class="pr-4">facts from long ago still hold capacity</td><td class="font-semibold">the gate (parts 3 and 4)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Keeping them apart matters more than it sounds. I originally wrote that the delta rule fixes the
      second one. It does not, and believing it did hid what the gate is actually for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 p-4">
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-1">Next in the series</p>
    <p class="text-lg font-semibold mb-1"><a href="/blog/delta-rule" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Part 2 — The delta rule</a></p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Writing to the same key twice adds instead of replacing. One change to the update rule fixes that exactly — and does nothing at all for crowding, which is worth being precise about.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-3">References</h2>
    <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm space-y-1 list-disc list-inside">
      <li>Kimi Team. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26692" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Kimi Linear: An Expressive, Efficient Attention Architecture</a>, arXiv:2510.26692, 2025.</li>
      <li>Katharopoulos et al. <i>Transformers are RNNs</i>, ICML 2020 — linear attention.</li>
